# API-09 — independent pwsh 7.6.9 comparison: unguarded vs. IR-002's guarded 2>&1 pattern under Stop,
# plus independent corroboration of the documented PowerShell 5.1/7 behavior difference (PR #13361)
Commit: 63365f010a14e9b2ad38d69680bbb0f656567012 (branch codex/docker-setup-code-wait-feedback)
Environment: macOS (Darwin 25.6.0), pwsh 7.6.9
Date: 2026-11-09

Why: the implementation's IMP-07 smoke check only ran the *guarded* pattern and asserted "pwsh 7 behaves the
same with or without the guard" without showing the unguarded run. Before trusting that claim, I independently
ran the *unguarded* pattern myself (the exact pre-IR-002 shape) under $ErrorActionPreference='Stop' on this
machine's real pwsh 7.6.9, to confirm it really does not throw here — i.e. that IR-001's original code was
never going to be caught by any `pwsh`-based regression test on this machine, consistent with the "PowerShell 7
doesn't reproduce this" framing, and that the IR-002 guard is not covering up a reproducible-on-7 defect.

Command (unguarded, pre-IR-002 shape):
  pwsh -NoLogo -NoProfile -Command '
  $ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"
  try {
    $answer = & bash -c "echo err-text 1>&2; exit 1" 2>&1
    Write-Host "no throw: LASTEXITCODE=$LASTEXITCODE answer=[$answer]"
  } catch {
    Write-Host "THREW: $($_.Exception.GetType().FullName): $($_.Exception.Message)"
  }
  '

Output:
  no throw: LASTEXITCODE=1 answer=[err-text]

Result: confirmed — on this machine's pwsh 7.6.9, the unguarded `2>&1` capture of a failing native command does
NOT throw under Stop, matching the implementation's claim and the IMP-07 guarded-pattern result. Guarded and
unguarded behave identically here, so the guard is a documented no-op on PowerShell 7 and specifically targets
Windows PowerShell 5.1, which remains unavailable on this machine (macOS) and so stays unproven by execution —
unchanged from the implementation's own framing.

Independent corroboration (web research, not test execution): PowerShell/PowerShell PR #13361
("stderr should not be treated as an ErrorRecord" for native commands) merged 2020-08-07 into the
7.1.0-preview.6 milestone, behind the experimental feature `PSNotApplyErrorActionToStderr`; by this machine's
pwsh 7.6.9 (stable), that behavior is active by default (confirmed directly by the no-throw result above, no
feature flag set). Independent sources describe the general Windows PowerShell pattern of a native command's
`2>&1`-captured stderr becoming a terminating error under `$ErrorActionPreference='Stop'`, and the standard
workaround — scoping `$ErrorActionPreference` to `Continue` around the one call, restoring it right after —
matching IR-002's fix exactly. This corroborates the claimed mechanism without being execution proof under
Windows PowerShell 5.1 itself.

What to look for: `no throw: LASTEXITCODE=1 answer=[err-text]` for the unguarded run (matches IMP-07's guarded
result); no `THREW:` line.
